+## Prospective component runtime Stage 2 clarification (UNAPPLIED)
+
+This is a prospective decision text. It installs nothing, creates no selector, and does not authorize a job. Existing K2 authority and historical component decisions retain their exact subjects.
+
+1. `ComponentHostSelector.acceptance_commit` is non-authorizing historical metadata. The current implementation does not verify Git ancestry or human provenance through that field. Its syntactic commit shape must not be described as provenance validation. The actual authority boundary remains the protected selector, byte-bound record/decision/independent-review chain and genuine human subject/reply. A future Git-provenance policy would require a separate reviewed implementation and decision; it cannot be inferred from this metadata. The schema description now states this explicitly without changing historical JSON shape.
+2. Component preparation requires `B-component-prospective-v3-selection/2`. Selection/1 is refused, including when all other bindings are internally consistent. No K2 compatibility change or implicit migration follows. Historical selections and receipts remain evidence under their original consumer versions.
+3. D3 successor uses inventory/2, record/2, selector/2, human-decision/2 and review/2. Full exact `{code,message,pointer}` matching is mandatory. The new expectation provenance binding identifies the independent per-case condition, source guard and inherited receipt. A null pointer, exact message or zero runtime entries alone does not prove arbitrary guard reach. Native-entry instrumentation and envelope consumption remain separate source-bound observations. This proposal does not introduce a new native diagnostic law. The full and delta patches in this directory reconstruct the same prospective module; neither is installed.
+4. Before an **activation source freeze**, A and B must review the concrete campaign/public-runtime adapter, D3 integration if authorized, startup launcher/manifest generation, and execution environment. Private file-probe transport is not the public EOF adapter. B's Stage 2 harness is private and is not public campaign authority. The proposed 120-definition/1344-call schedule is a separate pending decision; historical 124/1368 selections are unchanged.
+5. Use the accepted digest-pinned image for the native process, with no network, read-only root, explicit mounts, dropped capabilities, no-new-privileges, CPU1/RAM1GiB/PID64/FD256 and explicit bounded I/O/deadlines. This follows the existing private native execution environment. Do not transfer accepted-image ELF/DSO evidence to an untested host native launch. Host-side Python must have its own immutable interpreter/dependency/DSO inventory and reviewed launch chain. The existing public host executable mechanism must be reconciled with the chosen image transport in an exact unapplied change before activation.
+6. The Python launch form is externally pinned `PYTHON -I -S -B component_trusted_start.py MANIFEST EXPECTED_DIGEST ENTRY [ARGS...]`. The external launcher supplies the expected manifest digest; an arbitrary caller digest is not authority. Explicit canonical roots include project, dependencies and stdlib only. The finder compiles pinned Python source directly, checks pinned extension bytes, rejects unlisted modules/paths and closes path/meta hooks. Source/origin checks remain in component preparation. Refresh checks pins and loader/path state; immutable preparation additionally requires the startup state. This in-process state check is defense in depth, not an attestation against arbitrary pre-start code.
+7. The externally trusted immutable startup base comprises the actual interpreter, this bootstrap, dynamic loader/DSOs and initial stdlib/import machinery executed before manifest validation, including any bootstrap bytecode caches. `-B` suppresses writes only. A deployment must bind those bootstrap bytes independently (or eliminate caches and prove the alternative); the manifest does not retrospectively authenticate bootstrap execution. Trusted modules may execute code; this mechanism is not a Python sandbox. No editable `.pth` path, sitecustomize, usercustomize, sourceless bytecode or caller import hook is admitted for project consumers. The current host-side private tests do not establish the full immutable deployment chain. A-F3 remains DEFERRED for the stated verification/activation dependencies.
+8. Before the **activation build**, freeze an exact read-capture plan against its actual generated identity, compiler/linker/container/runtime mounts and tool inputs. Capture compiler/build-script/linker/loader reads, environment and generated inputs; retain start/end/status and every undecodable/unclosed record. Independently reconcile all observed reads to the immutable closure and account for capture loss. Missing coverage or unresolved records keep `read_audit_complete=false`. Do not rebuild repeatedly to seek a clean trace. A changed adapter, startup/preparation tool, consumer or environment affecting BuildInputs requires a new identity and actual build receipts; generated outputs are not source inputs, and later receipts never authorize themselves.
+
+The current cc899d289c84aa837cd72ca23956c29443a2af40 construction-read coverage is **NONE CAPTURED**. The three undecodable candidate-03 records remain historical unresolved evidence. They are not cc899d2 trace records, and source/cache/ELF/import checks do not substitute for an execution read capture. Neither fact can be repaired by reconstructing missing historical events.
+
+Root placement, activation build identity, real compiled authority/profile/HostRecord/selector, public campaign, D3 installation and exact human decisions remain later gates. Genuine H-L1-GEMM-01/H-L1-DMA-01 workings and U0024 are unchanged. The historical /2 same-engine/same-instant acquired-release restriction remains in force regardless of direction, including unclassified cases; /2 is neither repaired nor waived. No public performance qualification, artifact adoption, R/TPU execution, badge promotion or U4/G3a closure follows.
